00:04:07.226 --> 00:04:11.170
But one of the things that I've tried to
think a lot about at the highest level is,

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:12.389
where are we aiming for?

00:04:12.389 --> 00:04:15.327
When we built this evaluation system and
the application,

00:04:15.327 --> 00:04:18.102
what was really the end goal
of what we're trying to do?

00:04:18.102 --> 00:04:21.177
And if we get very simplistic for
anybody listening,

00:04:21.177 --> 00:04:25.620
hopefully this will help you as
you're writing your own applications.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:30.150
There's really three things that I think
are most important to us at Stanford.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.570
And one is,
we're an educational institution.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:33.906
So, the number one thing is,

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:37.750
we're trying to find students who
are going to be engaged in that classroom.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:41.708
And maybe all of you can reflect for
a moment, go back to university or

00:04:41.708 --> 00:04:43.100
even high school.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:46.260
Was there a class that you just
loved that you felt alive and

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.480
where everyone's hand was up?

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:51.480
And what was the teacher like and
how did you show up?

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:55.860
That's what the admissions team is trying
to find, is students who will come into

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.237
our classrooms and not only share their
own perspectives, but be this curious and

00:05:00.237 --> 00:05:03.960
excited to learn, and
our faculty it will feed on itself.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.077
So one of the priorities I'm always
holding as we design our application and

00:05:08.077 --> 00:05:11.354
process is, how do we make sure
we're getting that data, so

00:05:11.354 --> 00:05:15.720
we can find students who really
appreciate learning-for-learnings sake?

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:17.541
So that's one.
And then the second thing,

00:05:17.541 --> 00:05:21.505
Will already mentioned, our tagline of
change lives, change organizations,

00:05:21.505 --> 00:05:23.360
change the world.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.980
In some ways that can sound, I don't know,
high and mighty, like too hard to achieve.

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:32.652
The reality is, every time we pick
up an application, we're looking for

00:05:32.652 --> 00:05:36.340
people who will have positive impact
when they go out there and graduate.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.340
And it's actually hard.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:38.947
We struggle,

00:05:38.947 --> 00:05:42.550
we refine this application all the time
because it's not as if we're in a company.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:45.740
Let's say you're in consulting right now,
and you interview,

00:05:45.740 --> 00:05:49.450
there's some skills that someone
who's a consultant needs to have.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:54.148
For us, we want leaders to go out across
the globe, in every industry and whether

00:05:54.148 --> 00:05:59.350
it's for profit or non-profit, education,
could be journalism, media, finance.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:03.658
So, how do we find those characteristics
that really help people have

00:06:03.658 --> 00:06:06.881
leadership impact no matter
what they aspire to do?

00:06:06.881 --> 00:06:09.842
Or I love whoever said that they want to
build a company that will spread love

00:06:09.842 --> 00:06:10.431
in the world.

00:06:10.431 --> 00:06:11.865
How do I find just that person,

00:06:11.865 --> 00:06:14.830
who's got the capability to
spread love in the world?

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:17.778
So I'd say that's the second
thing that we're looking for,

00:06:17.778 --> 00:06:22.050
is this evidence that that you will go out
and impact the world in a positive way.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.875
And then third and
really important for Stanford, so

00:06:24.875 --> 00:06:27.782
I'm reminded of this often,
our community is small.

00:06:27.782 --> 00:06:31.930
What you'll hear our alumni and
students and faculty say is that,

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:36.764
it's a place where in Silicon Valley,
where our goal is to lift everyone up.

00:06:36.764 --> 00:06:41.963
It's to fail and fail often, but
to really, be thinking beyond oneself.

00:06:41.963 --> 00:06:46.676
And so, I'm looking when I read
applications to find evidence of,

00:06:46.676 --> 00:06:51.471
and to ask you data about, how I'm
hoping to see that you will come and

00:06:51.471 --> 00:06:53.468
strengthen our community.

00:06:53.468 --> 00:06:58.971
That you will be someone who, again,
will not only develop themselves,

00:06:58.971 --> 00:07:04.501
but help in the development of others,
and you can do that in so many ways.

00:07:04.501 --> 00:07:07.277
But that's what's special about Stanford,
is we're all working together.

00:07:07.277 --> 00:07:11.321
And I think it's special about innovation
and entrepreneurship in the Valley,

00:07:11.321 --> 00:07:15.200
that ecosphere we come from is really,
that we solve things with each other.

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:16.480
You can't do it alone.

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:20.459
So I'd say, Will,
those at the very top, are my goals.

00:07:20.459 --> 00:07:26.016
And then it's not surprising that when
we publish on the web what we look for,

00:07:26.016 --> 00:07:27.222
it maps to that.

00:07:27.222 --> 00:07:30.519
You'll see, and I'm sure if you've
attended another session, we talked about

00:07:30.519 --> 00:07:33.840
intellectual vitality, that's engagement,
when you'd be in the classroom.

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:38.462
We talked about demonstrated leadership
potential, which is really the past impact

00:07:38.462 --> 00:07:42.587
you've had, so what have you done out
there in the world that we can look to?

00:07:42.587 --> 00:07:44.122
And then personal qualities and
contributions?

00:07:44.122 --> 00:07:48.772
So, how have you gone through the world?

00:07:48.772 --> 00:07:51.960
What are your values, the choices
you've made, how you've shown up to.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:54.876
And from that,
we're looking at what that would mean for

00:07:54.876 --> 00:07:58.440
you to come to our community and
help us be stronger.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.782
Yeah there's been a phrase
that I used once and

00:08:01.782 --> 00:08:06.726
somehow it stuck background agnostic,
context dependent.

00:08:06.726 --> 00:08:07.820
&gt;&gt; I love it.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:09.430
&gt;&gt; Out there.-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:10.580
&gt;&gt; A little bit more.

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:15.024
But I know in the past I've had questions
from people like I'm from a non

00:08:15.024 --> 00:08:19.860
traditional background or I'm from,
like, how do I demonstrate, right?

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:23.979
And so as you think about intellectual
vitality, like that can come in so

00:08:23.979 --> 00:08:29.200
many different facets of your life and for
us, it doesn't matter where it shows up.

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:32.439
We celebrate it where it does and
then context right like,

00:08:32.439 --> 00:08:36.650
how it's going to show up maybe in
the individual who's a consultant.

00:08:36.650 --> 00:08:40.270
It might look very different from someone
who's helping lead a family business.

00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:42.820
So just know that for folks out there.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:45.580
It is about you and
like the world around you.

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:50.824
&gt;&gt; And would it be helpful to even go one
level deeper, deeper on that level and

00:08:50.824 --> 00:08:52.810
explains so dive down.

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:56.710
I'd love that you brought up that one
first is intellectual vitality because

00:08:56.710 --> 00:08:59.770
with the other ones that might
be easier for us to say, well,

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:01.520
that's what we're looking for.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:03.524
So then when we built the application,

00:09:03.524 --> 00:09:06.330
