00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:19.856
So rest considering that in its entirety
and a little bit of a different way,

00:13:19.856 --> 00:13:24.929
but there's more important written
information put in one place or

00:13:24.929 --> 00:13:29.170
another, because it will vary so
much by person.

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:33.056
So I would not get hung up at all about
how is person going to read my particular

00:13:33.056 --> 00:13:33.860
application.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:36.872
I would be thinking much more
about how can I make sure that she

00:13:36.872 --> 00:13:39.900
knows Knows that I'm curious,
she knows about my stories.

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:43.260
I'm most proud of the impact,
what I've done and also,

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:47.170
she knows who I am as a person,
he or she, I should say.

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:50.710
Okay, when I read applications,
I'll try to think of some examples.

00:13:52.490 --> 00:13:54.880
With real examples, and
I've read thousands.

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:56.843
So hopefully this will be easy, Will.

00:13:56.843 --> 00:14:00.731
So usually we'll start at the very
beginning of the application you're

00:14:00.731 --> 00:14:03.940
going to fill out just to
get a sense of who you are.

00:14:03.940 --> 00:14:06.753
In terms of where you're born and
where you're going to school and

00:14:06.753 --> 00:14:09.820
some that biographical information,
but oftentimes even from there,

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:12.200
I'm taking leaps into other
parts of the application.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:15.680
So for instance, maybe I'm getting
that you were you went to school and

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:20.160
what you studied and you studied classics,
and then I see you're at Citibank.

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:22.160
And that'll be like, wow,
how did that happen?

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:24.149
And before I want to learn
more about Citibank,

00:14:24.149 --> 00:14:27.210
I'll probably jump to your transcript,
and say, what did you study?

00:14:27.210 --> 00:14:29.860
Did you have any finance or quant courses?

00:14:29.860 --> 00:14:33.860
Because holistic to use your phrase,
the way that you perform, or what you

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:38.052
worked on at Citi may be very different
from someone in the beginning at least of

00:14:38.052 --> 00:14:42.331
your career Then someone who spent four
years as a business or economics major.

00:14:42.331 --> 00:14:46.442
Sso that might be a good example of
how we're constantly asking ourselves

00:14:46.442 --> 00:14:50.684
questions or maybe if I see in your in
that information about college that you

00:14:50.684 --> 00:14:54.618
you paid for a significant part of
your college or that your first gen.

00:14:54.618 --> 00:14:57.769
I might immediately jump to
your essay then think, wow,

00:14:57.769 --> 00:15:01.973
that must have been really hard but
there are other things in your context and

00:15:01.973 --> 00:15:06.372
how you've grown up that might shed light
or a lens on the choices you've made or

00:15:06.372 --> 00:15:09.812
the transcript or
the classes that you've taken all of that.

00:15:09.812 --> 00:15:12.610
So it'll be really individual for
each person.

00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:16.214
But I would think about telling
us from your perspective,

00:15:16.214 --> 00:15:21.155
how can you be most strategic to answer
those goals those three goals I gave you.

00:15:21.155 --> 00:15:22.511
Was that helpful, Will?

00:15:22.511 --> 00:15:23.775
Hope I helped you out here?

00:15:23.775 --> 00:15:24.668
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and

00:15:24.668 --> 00:15:26.790
I think that also starts
to come into play.

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:28.655
There's a question from Bruce.

00:15:28.655 --> 00:15:30.169
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; About the admissions, the values.

00:15:30.169 --> 00:15:35.170
That the admissions committee holds and
what guides our decision making process.

00:15:35.170 --> 00:15:38.061
And I think for us,
it is that understanding and

00:15:38.061 --> 00:15:41.979
not just the projection of ideas and
notions but also evidence.

00:15:41.979 --> 00:15:46.568
So every time we're reading we're
looking for in the application.

00:15:46.568 --> 00:15:49.307
Here's where intellectual
vitality like some form

00:15:49.307 --> 00:15:52.030
of intellectual vitality
might come to life.

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:55.980
And maybe it is in your writing,
maybe it's in the letter of reference.

00:15:55.980 --> 00:16:00.026
So I think That evidence piece of it,
kind of on the tactical practical.

00:16:00.026 --> 00:16:04.326
The better folks are able to articulate,
not just the what, but the how.

00:16:04.326 --> 00:16:08.987
I think that's one of those on
the practical side of things things

00:16:08.987 --> 00:16:13.392
that help guide us and, okay,
let's make a coherent, yes,

00:16:13.392 --> 00:16:17.120
look to admit kind of decision evidence.

00:16:17.120 --> 00:16:19.578
&gt;&gt; We're looking for
those patterns of evidence.

00:16:19.578 --> 00:16:23.708
And I want to caution as you're thinking
about in the next five weeks, for

00:16:23.708 --> 00:16:28.610
those of you who are thinking first round,
putting together your application.

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:33.602
I love what Will said about being agnostic
about where you've done it in the context.

00:16:33.602 --> 00:16:35.136
What I would start with is,

00:16:35.136 --> 00:16:39.680
when we think about especially that
demonstrated leadership potential piece,

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:44.460
what are the things that you've done
in your life that you're most proud of?

00:16:44.460 --> 00:16:48.697
So if we get to sit together tomorrow,
we're having a cup of coffee, and

00:16:48.697 --> 00:16:50.438
I get to ask you the question.

00:16:50.438 --> 00:16:54.341
Bruce, tell me about a time that you've
had an impact that was meaningful to you,

00:16:54.341 --> 00:16:57.056
really thinking,
what have I done that I've worked on,

00:16:57.056 --> 00:16:58.870
not what's important to Kirsten.

00:16:58.870 --> 00:17:02.926
because I speak over and over with
prospects who think that they needed to be

00:17:02.926 --> 00:17:07.462
the president of the student body, or
they needed to go to a certain school, or

00:17:07.462 --> 00:17:09.390
a certain kind of company.

00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:11.949
And the reality is going
back to that last example,

00:17:11.949 --> 00:17:15.638
if you're the first person in your
family who was able to go to college, or

00:17:15.638 --> 00:17:20.030
you ended up having to pay for a third of
your college, to me, that's leadership.

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:21.040
That's persistence.

00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:21.774
That's grit.

00:17:21.774 --> 00:17:22.787
That's taking risk.

00:17:22.787 --> 00:17:24.180
That's showing initiative.

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:28.303
So if you start with yourself because you
understand your context better than any of

00:17:28.303 --> 00:17:31.020
us could,
don't project what we want to hear.

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:32.880
What have you been working really hard on?

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:36.020
And I believe each of us has
some stories that are powerful.

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:39.784
And just making sure, at least for
Stanford, we can't speak for other

00:17:39.784 --> 00:17:44.192
schools, but at Stanford, that's where
we're seeing leadership come to life.

00:17:46.213 --> 00:17:50.842
&gt;&gt; And so, as we're talking about the
application and how we can put our best

00:17:50.842 --> 00:17:56.130
foot forward, this whole week has been
about tips, advice, best practices.

00:17:56.130 --> 00:17:58.354
So is it okay if we go in that direction,
Kirsten?

00:17:58.354 --> 00:18:03.201
Good, knowing that between the two of us,
thousands of applications,

00:18:03.201 --> 00:18:06.140
and even talking to our teammates, right,

00:18:06.140 --> 00:18:10.599
I want to say there's at least
a century of admissions experience.

00:18:10.599 --> 00:18:13.807
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; And so

00:18:13.807 --> 00:18:18.490
hopefully some of our observations
come with some backing there.

00:18:18.490 --> 00:18:24.610
But this week, we split even our workshops
into how do you start your story?

00:18:24.610 --> 00:18:27.166
How do you communicate it
in the written format?

00:18:27.166 --> 00:18:30.675
And then when it comes to speaking,
because speaking is also going to be,

00:18:30.675 --> 00:18:33.120
not just part of the application process,
but

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:35.900
a consistent part of
the journey throughout.

00:18:35.900 --> 00:18:39.354
So maybe starting at that
starting your story, so for

00:18:39.354 --> 00:18:42.733
folks who might be thinking
about business school or

00:18:42.733 --> 00:18:47.357
really trying to put together the threads
and the details of maybe why?

00:18:47.357 --> 00:18:52.221
Do you have any tips or advice for folks
and things that they might consider as

00:18:52.221 --> 00:18:57.320
they're pulling together the rationale for
business school or for Stanford?

00:18:57.320 --> 00:18:59.996
&gt;&gt; So that why Stanford question,
is that what you're speaking about?

00:18:59.996 --> 00:19:03.730
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
just broadly as people are preparing so.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:08.928
&gt;&gt; Yeah, really, when I think about the
essays and short answers you're writing,

00:19:08.928 --> 00:19:13.160
for me they fall into those two camps,
which is what have you done?

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:17.256
And we just talked about that, that would
be really starting with what are you proud

00:19:17.256 --> 00:19:19.200
of the impact that you've had?

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:21.637
And I'm going to pause there again too,

00:19:21.637 --> 00:19:25.530
because this impact could be
very individual to you as well.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:28.387
Maybe you're a violinist and
you worked really hard and

00:19:28.387 --> 00:19:30.769
you performed in ensembles or
in symphonies.

00:19:30.769 --> 00:19:34.773
That may be the way that you're
going to demonstrate leadership to us.

00:19:34.773 --> 00:19:38.658
Or maybe it's in your church, or your
mosque, or with your family, helping them,

00:19:38.658 --> 00:19:40.750
there's so
many ways that you can show that.

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:44.152
But think about what you've done,
that's where I would start first, and

00:19:44.152 --> 00:19:45.180
reflect and jot down.

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:50.108
And I use three stories because I
want you to kind of have a chance to

00:19:50.108 --> 00:19:54.951
play with that and brainstorm,
think outside of the box.

00:19:54.951 --> 00:19:58.767
because it may be something that you're
proud of that you wouldn't necessarily

00:19:58.767 --> 00:20:02.010
have led with, if that makes sense,
so that's part A.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:05.668
The second part that we talked about
was how you've walked through life,

00:20:05.668 --> 00:20:08.800
who you are as a person,
what are your values and motivations?

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:12.397
And our essay about what matters most and
why gets to the absolute heart.

00:20:12.397 --> 00:20:17.664
And it's probably one of the hardest
essays you're likely to answer,

00:20:17.664 --> 00:20:19.875
[LAUGH] that's the hard news.

00:20:19.875 --> 00:20:25.063
For me, you've probably
gotten a lot of advice, but

00:20:25.063 --> 00:20:30.615
for me personally,
if I had to put in my two cents on that.

00:20:30.615 --> 00:20:34.332
If you could look back over the last,
since college, for many of you,

00:20:34.332 --> 00:20:35.533
that's eight years.

00:20:35.533 --> 00:20:38.156
You've made choices,
you've decided what to major in, and

00:20:38.156 --> 00:20:41.494
you've decided what extracurricular
activities you've been involved in.

00:20:41.494 --> 00:20:45.396
You've decided what types of
organizations to work for

00:20:45.396 --> 00:20:49.141
post potentially,
if you have had work experience.

00:20:49.141 --> 00:20:52.696
And at the time, you may not have thought
very much about why you're making

00:20:52.696 --> 00:20:55.575
these choices, but
now you have an opportunity to go back and

00:20:55.575 --> 00:20:58.300
connect the dots and
see what those things are.

00:20:58.300 --> 00:21:00.515
I know I was working or
speaking with a candidate recently and

00:21:00.515 --> 00:21:03.140
they're telling me about what
they had done in college.

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:06.684
And they had been an RA in their
college dorm, they had been a TA for

00:21:06.684 --> 00:21:10.000
a teacher in some courses,
they'd done that twice.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:13.100
They had signed up to be
a wellness counselor.

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:17.308
And immediately we're seeing this theme
that whether it was in academics, or

00:21:17.308 --> 00:21:18.710
in living situations, or

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:22.297
even on the wellness side of just
helping people develop and grow.

00:21:22.297 --> 00:21:25.004
And that's like, well,
there you go, that's a big aha.

00:21:25.004 --> 00:21:27.070
So maybe you can look
over your choices and

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:30.627
think about what's been important to you,
and why you've made them.

00:21:30.627 --> 00:21:35.173
And you will start to understand
what those deeper values are.

00:21:35.173 --> 00:21:38.647
So that would be the advice
I'd give on the essay as well.

00:21:38.647 --> 00:21:43.076
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
[LAUGH] there's a kind of preparation tip,

00:21:43.076 --> 00:21:50.211
process that I shared with people,
remember, reflect, process, connect.

00:21:50.211 --> 00:21:51.939
&gt;&gt; Love it, yeah.
&gt;&gt; Just remember the things you did,

00:21:51.939 --> 00:21:54.190
reflect on the circumstances around it.

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:58.720
Process how did that kind of matter and
why was it significant?

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:02.217
And then connect the dots between
the different parts of your life and

00:22:02.217 --> 00:22:03.977
how might it inform your journey.

00:22:03.977 --> 00:22:07.517
So I was trying to put it in a catchy
phrase that people could remember if

00:22:07.517 --> 00:22:11.357
they're like, okay, if I need to sit down,
what's an easy way to think and

00:22:11.357 --> 00:22:13.823
prepare with some of
the things that I've done?

00:22:13.823 --> 00:22:16.231
So, kind of plugging
that pneumonic in there.

00:22:16.231 --> 00:22:19.041
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I love it, I totally love it,
I love your pneumonic.

00:22:19.041 --> 00:22:21.695
So I'm going to steal that next
time I'm in another group.

00:22:21.695 --> 00:22:22.572
&gt;&gt; Do it.

00:22:22.572 --> 00:22:25.337
&gt;&gt; Maybe what's also important
is to go back to the why.

00:22:25.337 --> 00:22:26.940
Why does Stanford ask this question?

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:30.000
We've been asking it at least for two
decades, and I'm not going to change it.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.820
So if you're going to apply in a year or
two,

00:22:31.820 --> 00:22:34.220
you'll still have the luxury
of doing this question.

00:22:35.710 --> 00:22:41.737
There has been tremendous research on
looking at leaders later in their journey,

00:22:41.737 --> 00:22:48.240
what prompted their impact or prompted the
results that they were able to achieve?

00:22:48.240 --> 00:22:52.538
And one of the distinctions that we see is
leaders who know what their values are and

00:22:52.538 --> 00:22:56.711
can speak to their motivation, tend to
use that as the energy to take risks, and

00:22:56.711 --> 00:22:57.850
to take initiative.

00:22:57.850 --> 00:23:02.991
So we're looking for you to reflect
on what could those motivations be,

00:23:02.991 --> 00:23:06.870
and that will help guide
yourself in the future.

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:10.574
Now Will and I are not going to
evaluate what values you have,

00:23:10.574 --> 00:23:12.772
so this is a projective question.

00:23:12.772 --> 00:23:15.853
For us, it's more about your
self-awareness and reflection, and

00:23:15.853 --> 00:23:17.829
knowing who you are and
could be as a leader.

00:23:17.829 --> 00:23:19.657
So that when you come to Stanford,

00:23:19.657 --> 00:23:23.590
you're ready to take advantage of
all the opportunities we have here.

00:23:23.590 --> 00:23:25.023
And go ahead, Will say.

00:23:25.023 --> 00:23:28.871
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no, it's almost like you're
kickstarting that leadership development

00:23:28.871 --> 00:23:30.272
and leadership curriculum.

00:23:30.272 --> 00:23:34.492
Because there's so much introspection
that's required to answer,

00:23:34.492 --> 00:23:36.870
why should someone follow you?

00:23:36.870 --> 00:23:41.280
Or why should someone want to
follow you to be even more precise?

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:43.722
I really appreciate the question.

00:23:43.722 --> 00:23:48.824
I've mentioned it before, I often use
it as a kind of way to make decisions

00:23:48.824 --> 00:23:53.350
when I met pivot points in life,
so even beyond the application,

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:57.384
I think it's Embracing
the process of applying,

00:23:57.384 --> 00:24:01.454
there are some greater learnings
that can be had oftentimes, and

00:24:01.454 --> 00:24:05.880
the way I grew up, it's like you apply for
a thing and then that's it.

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:09.160
Whereas in this process,
there's also, it's an invitation for

00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:13.026
greater learnings that I never realized
could have been part of something like

00:24:13.026 --> 00:24:15.160
applying to school before.

00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:17.180
Yeah, and I love that.

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:19.850
And it's also our second essay,
why Stanford?

00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:22.280
What are your aspirations and
how will you get there?

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:23.654
It's the same.

00:24:23.654 --> 00:24:28.616
We're asking you on the one hand to look
back at what your motivations were, and

00:24:28.616 --> 00:24:32.984
then to use those to tell us what you
might be interested in working on or

00:24:32.984 --> 00:24:35.360
solving in your future.

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:38.970
I think it's worth saying in this
is different than other schools.

00:24:38.970 --> 00:24:41.720
So I really want to make sure
we're just speaking for Stanford.

00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:45.560
We don't have Career Services
sitting on our admissions committee.

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:49.745
We really believe if you get into Stanford
that you are capable of what achieving

00:24:49.745 --> 00:24:54.570
whatever dreams and aspirations you have,
that won't happen necessarily immediately.

00:24:54.570 --> 00:24:56.693
Maybe there are pivots or
expertise you have to gain but

00:24:56.693 --> 00:24:58.400
we know that you'll get there.

00:24:58.400 --> 00:25:02.730
So you should feel free to write about
whatever you want in those aspirations.

00:25:02.730 --> 00:25:04.706
And we also know that
those are going to change,

00:25:04.706 --> 00:25:08.294
the vast majority of our students think
they'll do something when they come in and

00:25:08.294 --> 00:25:12.150
we'll do something very different when
they graduate and that's okay, too.

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:16.170
So again, this is a question to say, have
you thought enough about what you care

00:25:16.170 --> 00:25:19.950
about, that you can have a true north,
even though that might shift west or

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:22.129
east and you'll go through this journey.

00:25:22.129 --> 00:25:24.985
But we're really curious
about at this moment in time,

00:25:24.985 --> 00:25:28.900
what's important to you and
what would you like to work on?

00:25:28.900 --> 00:25:31.972
Yeah, because even if it's not the first
thing after there's probably going to be

00:25:31.972 --> 00:25:33.364
at least a few pivots in your life.

00:25:33.364 --> 00:25:37.199
This is just one stop in a career and

00:25:37.199 --> 00:25:42.130
life basically that extends beyond.

00:25:42.130 --> 00:25:45.109
Yes, you will take the memories,
and community and

00:25:45.109 --> 00:25:49.645
the learnings from the GSD with you, but
It's really to empower you to be able to

00:25:49.645 --> 00:25:54.050
thrive regardless of your circumstance and
situation, I think.

00:25:54.050 --> 00:25:54.550
Yeah.

00:25:55.650 --> 00:26:01.500
Right, and then so we often talk
a lot about the writing, the essays.

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:06.355
An area that I know we both find
fascinating that we don't get to talk

00:26:06.355 --> 00:26:08.748
too much about is the interview.

00:26:08.748 --> 00:26:09.290
Yeah.

00:26:09.290 --> 00:26:12.910
And I know you have a really
rich history and assessment.

00:26:12.910 --> 00:26:17.779
Can you talk us through like the Stanford
behavioral based interview, any

00:26:17.779 --> 00:26:23.740
advice that you might give for folks and
how to think about, what it looks like?

00:26:23.740 --> 00:26:25.624
Yeah, yes well, for us,

00:26:25.624 --> 00:26:31.660
we will interview somewhere between
10 to 15% of our applicants.

00:26:31.660 --> 00:26:34.550
So if you get the email inviting
you to interview, congratulations.

00:26:34.550 --> 00:26:36.048
That's a wonderful step in the process.

00:26:36.048 --> 00:26:40.090
Our interviews are done by
alumni across the globe.

00:26:40.090 --> 00:26:43.390
So they'll have your resume and
not your whole application.

00:26:43.390 --> 00:26:47.820
So it's your chance to share with them
open a little bit more detail their story.

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:50.120
But let's go back to your question well,

00:26:50.120 --> 00:26:53.946
why do we structure the way that our
interviews and the way that we do?

00:26:53.946 --> 00:26:56.744
Their behavioral based
structural interviews and

00:26:56.744 --> 00:27:01.101
what they're meant to do is to really take
this application which is enough flat

00:27:01.101 --> 00:27:03.050
piece of paper to bring it to life.

00:27:03.050 --> 00:27:06.750
It's your opportunity to really
bring it to living color.

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:11.502
And we basically will ask you the same
questions that you're answering in your

00:27:11.502 --> 00:27:12.830
short answers.

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:16.063
Tell us about a time you've had had
an impact that was meaningful or tell us

00:27:16.063 --> 00:27:19.730
about a time you've had a challenge, tell
us about a time you've worked on a team.

00:27:19.730 --> 00:27:23.959
And notice that tell us about a time
language, that's very typical of

00:27:23.959 --> 00:27:28.127
behavioral based interviews for
those of you who have not done them.

00:27:28.127 --> 00:27:31.511
What we're trying to do here
is we believe that your past

00:27:31.511 --> 00:27:34.420
behavior will predict future behavior.

00:27:34.420 --> 00:27:38.251
So you had an opportunity your application
in the essays are short answers to tell us

00:27:38.251 --> 00:27:42.240
about some of those stories that you
think are important and you're proud of.

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:45.406
But when we're reading an application,
we don't have a chance to probe and

00:27:45.406 --> 00:27:47.060
really bring those to life.

00:27:47.060 --> 00:27:51.999
So what could be a good example to really,
I'm going to play, this is an interview

00:27:51.999 --> 00:27:57.320
will really make this concrete and the why
behind what we're trying to do here.

00:27:57.320 --> 00:28:00.650
Let's say that many of our
applicants come from finance.

00:28:00.650 --> 00:28:03.477
Maybe you were asked to help
build a training program or

00:28:03.477 --> 00:28:07.287
launch a new initiative on training for
new analysts that are coming in and

00:28:07.287 --> 00:28:11.830
you, right, you give us a short answer
question about that maybe 200 words.

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:16.200
So that's a really exciting leadership,
I call it impact moment.

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:18.936
But maybe your interviewer as your
when he asks you a question like,

00:28:18.936 --> 00:28:21.220
tell me about a time
you've taken initiative.

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:22.555
You leave with this example and

00:28:22.555 --> 00:28:25.900
the interviewer might want to know
well how many students attended that?

00:28:25.900 --> 00:28:27.590
Or how long was the training program?

00:28:27.590 --> 00:28:31.950
Or how did you get permission to
even to do it or the resources?

00:28:31.950 --> 00:28:34.540
Or how did you develop the curriculum?

00:28:34.540 --> 00:28:38.363
So all of these things we may or may not
have been able to ask you about in a flat

00:28:38.363 --> 00:28:41.670
application, but in your interview,
you can bring it to life.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:44.037
And I think, so
these interviews in some ways,

00:28:44.037 --> 00:28:46.534
you won't have a lot of
additional preparation.

00:28:46.534 --> 00:28:50.290
because you've already been thinking
about those important stories.

00:28:50.290 --> 00:28:54.360
But I want to make sure, because I
do some of the interviews as well.

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:59.212
One potential, what I call it or
tip something to avoid is make sure

00:28:59.212 --> 00:29:04.500
when you come to your interview,
you know that you're the hero.

00:29:04.500 --> 00:29:06.300
And heroes can be humble.

00:29:06.300 --> 00:29:10.680
So as you come in and really think about,
what was the situation, you saw there was

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:15.185
a problem and that new analysts weren't
getting the training they needed.

00:29:15.185 --> 00:29:16.395
What did you do about it?

00:29:16.395 --> 00:29:19.425
So what actions did you
as an individual take?

00:29:19.425 --> 00:29:20.427
Why was Is that important?

00:29:20.427 --> 00:29:22.982
Why was that meaningful
to the organization?

00:29:22.982 --> 00:29:27.212
So what happened from it and be willing
to step in that and be the hero.

00:29:27.212 --> 00:29:30.112
And again, heroes can be humble,
but you still need to own.

00:29:30.112 --> 00:29:33.482
This was what I did in this initiative.

00:29:33.482 --> 00:29:37.550
So that's the best way I think you
could prepare for our interviews.

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:40.265
I was laughing because I thought you were
going to ask me to like demo with you.

00:29:40.265 --> 00:29:42.441
I'm glad we did it, we did.

00:29:42.441 --> 00:29:44.780
Yeah, probably yesterday Yeah.

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:48.920
One of the tips I shared yesterday and
kind of ingesting the phrasing.

00:29:48.920 --> 00:29:53.139
There's no I and T but there isn't admit,
I was joking but the point was,

00:29:53.139 --> 00:29:58.191
even in a group setting, there's something
that you might have done as an individual.

00:29:58.191 --> 00:30:03.071
And there's something you didn't do as
an individual to help advance something

00:30:03.071 --> 00:30:04.517
from point A to point B.

00:30:04.517 --> 00:30:09.509
A lot of us it's hard to shift out
of that we mentality and embrace,

00:30:09.509 --> 00:30:11.870
and really center yourself.

00:30:11.870 --> 00:30:15.462
I know,
I remember one of our interviews Kirsten.

00:30:15.462 --> 00:30:18.166
No.
Even having done interview as myself,

00:30:18.166 --> 00:30:21.825
you're like, well you keep saying we,
what did you do?

00:30:21.825 --> 00:30:26.555
Even though it was an example, I knew,
you were actually involved and

00:30:26.555 --> 00:30:28.530
I was like, wait.

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:34.640
So definitely it's a real thing, and we
encourage and we want to know about you.

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:39.160
So just, we invite you to
center yourself in your attic.

00:30:40.200 --> 00:30:42.290
That's fantastic advice.

00:30:42.290 --> 00:30:43.420
Absolutely right.

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:46.968
And oftentimes, it took a group to get
things done, but just like you said, well,

00:30:46.968 --> 00:30:49.030
you were taking actions
that help that group.

00:30:49.030 --> 00:30:49.870
So speak to those.

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:54.076
And if we do probe,
it's because we want to learn more and

00:30:54.076 --> 00:30:56.661
we're trying to help you
get towards something.

00:30:56.661 --> 00:31:01.390
That will be helpful for us as we
understand your application as a whole.

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:05.770
And the other thing is,
we are blessed on our team.

00:31:07.010 --> 00:31:10.226
Carrie Oliver has been running
our interviews for a long time,

00:31:10.226 --> 00:31:14.473
she's trained a hundreds of alumni across
the globe, they love to participate and

00:31:14.473 --> 00:31:16.020
come back every year.

00:31:16.020 --> 00:31:19.768
So a part of this too is just to meet
someone who really cares about Stanford

00:31:19.768 --> 00:31:22.010
and who wants to get to know you and
you them.

00:31:22.010 --> 00:31:25.078
So once you're done with behavioral
based part of the interview,

00:31:25.078 --> 00:31:28.583
there'll be a wonderful moment at the end
where you can ask them questions,

00:31:28.583 --> 00:31:29.929
learn more about Stanford.

00:31:29.929 --> 00:31:34.219
And that's again, just the time to
be yourself, relax, develop rapport,

00:31:34.219 --> 00:31:35.613
get to know someone new.

00:31:35.613 --> 00:31:39.949
Yeah, Carrie has been such
a gift to the program,

00:31:39.949 --> 00:31:44.714
it has really shined,
I think about the recent years.

00:31:44.714 --> 00:31:46.453
Kind of shifting away
from the application now.

00:31:46.453 --> 00:31:49.993
So we've kind of moved through
the process, how do you think about it,

00:31:49.993 --> 00:31:52.235
how do you prepare,
we talked about essays,

00:31:52.235 --> 00:31:55.660
that interview which is at next step
as part of the holistic process.

00:31:55.660 --> 00:32:01.246
But again, this is just the one step along
the journey and We want to make sure that

00:32:01.246 --> 00:32:06.192
you're going to thrive at business school,
so having seen so many journeys through.

00:32:06.192 --> 00:32:10.762
Do you have any tips for folks to help
them hit the ground running once they're

00:32:10.762 --> 00:32:13.880
at school and
really maximize the opportunity?

00:32:13.880 --> 00:32:18.137
Kind of with the notion that also as
you're preparing for business school,

00:32:18.137 --> 00:32:22.010
that's what we're looking for
in the application process as well.

00:32:22.010 --> 00:32:26.497
So how can someone actually set
themself up for a long term success.

00:32:26.497 --> 00:32:30.100
And really think about the big picture
here and not just the application?

00:32:30.100 --> 00:32:33.961
&gt;&gt; Yeah, well, it's so easy to get caught
up with all these deadlines coming and

00:32:33.961 --> 00:32:36.550
essays and
everything else you just pick this done.

00:32:37.650 --> 00:32:41.853
We have designed their process
to try to help you reflect and

00:32:41.853 --> 00:32:45.180
be prepared for business school.

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:49.214
One thing I may say is this is,
most of you in the audience have had,

00:32:49.214 --> 00:32:52.762
I'm guessing you went to college,
had that experience.

00:32:52.762 --> 00:32:57.087
Now you've been working for several years
and there's been more of a path about,

00:32:57.087 --> 00:32:58.725
what do I have to do to succeed?

00:32:58.725 --> 00:33:01.854
And this is probably one of the first
times in your life where there are no

00:33:01.854 --> 00:33:03.376
more boxes to check necessarily.

00:33:03.376 --> 00:33:06.575
[LAUGH] The world is open for
you to figure out what do you care about,

00:33:06.575 --> 00:33:07.900
what do you want to work on?

00:33:07.900 --> 00:33:11.485
What's your own, how you're going to
find energy to show up everyday,

00:33:11.485 --> 00:33:13.960
and be excited about what you're doing.

00:33:13.960 --> 00:33:16.980
So it's a great,
as you think about this transition.

00:33:16.980 --> 00:33:20.317
Your job as you're coming to
business school is not to,

00:33:20.317 --> 00:33:24.010
obviously it's to do well in
the classroom and get involved.

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:26.823
But it's also to really uncover for
yourself,

00:33:26.823 --> 00:33:31.129
what will be most important to you
when you graduate to begin working on.

00:33:31.129 --> 00:33:35.408
And there are so many opportunities
at Stanford and other schools for

00:33:35.408 --> 00:33:38.814
you to sample whatever
expertise you want to develop.

00:33:38.814 --> 00:33:43.424
And it's across disciplines for us, so it
could be in any of the graduate schools.

00:33:43.424 --> 00:33:46.509
Whether you have an interest in
health care, education, or policy,

00:33:46.509 --> 00:33:47.940
the world is an oyster.

00:33:47.940 --> 00:33:50.881
So I hope during this time and
over the summer as you transition.

00:33:50.881 --> 00:33:54.343
One of the things you can do is begin
to think, what would be my priorities?

00:33:54.343 --> 00:33:56.436
What do I want to learn more about,

00:33:56.436 --> 00:34:00.205
what do I want to dive into even
though I know that will evolve?

00:34:00.205 --> 00:34:02.451
So when the world is completely open,

00:34:02.451 --> 00:34:06.252
I have some way to prioritize what
could be most important to me.

00:34:06.252 --> 00:34:09.717
And so I'd say coming up with a list
of what those, it's in your probably,

00:34:09.717 --> 00:34:13.132
what are your aspirations that's
probably helping you to get that list?

00:34:13.132 --> 00:34:15.023
So that's one, two rest,

00:34:15.023 --> 00:34:20.384
[LAUGH] what we hear from folks coming in
our first term is the classes are hard.

00:34:20.384 --> 00:34:23.100
And they take [LAUGH] a lot of time and
there's all the social things happening.

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:24.226
So really knowing,

00:34:24.226 --> 00:34:28.344
even though some of you may be in roles
right now where you work extra hard.

00:34:28.344 --> 00:34:31.310
I've heard from folks in finance,
they work weekends and all of this, but

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:33.536
I've heard from those same
students when they come in.

00:34:33.536 --> 00:34:36.917
That it was just as intensive that those
first few terms here in a different way,

00:34:36.917 --> 00:34:38.339
maybe a little more fun at times.

00:34:38.339 --> 00:34:42.929
But certainly intensive, and also
another tip is look at your quantitative

00:34:42.929 --> 00:34:45.880
preparation and as an applicant as well.

00:34:45.880 --> 00:34:50.240
So we will, some of you have had lots
of exposure to that in the past, for

00:34:50.240 --> 00:34:51.924
those of you who haven't.

00:34:51.924 --> 00:34:55.797
If you haven't taken statistics,
or economics, micro or

00:34:55.797 --> 00:34:57.973
macro economics, or calculus.

00:34:57.973 --> 00:35:01.868
Those will be core skills that you,
I shouldn't say calculus necessarily but

00:35:01.868 --> 00:35:03.710
some kind of higher level math.

00:35:03.710 --> 00:35:06.570
There's going to be skills
here that you'll need to rely

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:08.170
on even in that first term.

00:35:08.170 --> 00:35:09.987
So before you're applying,

00:35:09.987 --> 00:35:13.558
if you notice that you have not
had some of that classwork.

00:35:13.558 --> 00:35:16.549
You might want to speak in additional
information about what kind of

00:35:16.549 --> 00:35:19.711
prep you will be getting, something
like an MBA math could be helpful.

00:35:19.711 --> 00:35:21.716
But you want to make
sure when you get here,

00:35:21.716 --> 00:35:24.848
you have some understanding of
the fundamentals of those topics.

00:35:24.848 --> 00:35:29.755
And then I think lastly,
my most favorite thing to say is,

00:35:29.755 --> 00:35:34.372
we get so excited when I about
getting you to Stanford.

00:35:34.372 --> 00:35:36.886
And kind of falling in love with
your [LAUGH] application, and

00:35:36.886 --> 00:35:38.030
we can't wait to meet you.

00:35:39.190 --> 00:35:42.945
You also if you're selected,
there's so few spots, I guess I'd say.

00:35:42.945 --> 00:35:47.475
There's a responsibility to think about
how you're going to also as a leader show

00:35:47.475 --> 00:35:49.800
up and help build this community.

00:35:49.800 --> 00:35:51.972
And it's never too early,
you can start over the summer,

00:35:51.972 --> 00:35:53.574
think about your classes you want to take.

00:35:53.574 --> 00:35:57.152
But also what's going on in campus and
what problems, are not problems,

00:35:57.152 --> 00:35:57.855
I should say.

00:35:57.855 --> 00:36:01.987
But what are some of the ways that you
can become part of the community in

00:36:01.987 --> 00:36:02.698
a deep way?

00:36:02.698 --> 00:36:06.433
And that often means stepping in and
taking initiative to support others help

00:36:06.433 --> 00:36:09.270
others, so I'd love for
you if I could give that advice.

00:36:09.270 --> 00:36:13.079
To be thinking in the summer and
actually whatever program you go to,

00:36:13.079 --> 00:36:17.297
that this is a chance to start your own
sort of personal leadership journey.

00:36:17.297 --> 00:36:23.436
And begin to help and build others up over
the next two years in a really safe place.

00:36:23.436 --> 00:36:25.845
That will, and
those kinds of commitments and

00:36:25.845 --> 00:36:28.267
investments will last
again over a lifetime.

00:36:28.267 --> 00:36:31.532
You'll be part of the Stanford
community for 40 years [LAUGH] or 50.

00:36:31.532 --> 00:36:34.319
As long as you're alive, so
whatever connections and

00:36:34.319 --> 00:36:38.830
investment you're making now will pay
dividends for all of us in the future.

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:44.550
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I want to add maybe one more,
we want you to succeed and thrive.

00:36:44.550 --> 00:36:48.755
And that also means it's okay to ask for
help, like whether it's academical,

00:36:48.755 --> 00:36:51.347
because you might have been
out of school for a while.

00:36:51.347 --> 00:36:53.794
And it's a different type of engagement,
and

00:36:53.794 --> 00:36:55.938
intensity than maybe in your work life.

00:36:55.938 --> 00:37:00.369
So it might be academic help, there's
academic support, there's career help.

00:37:00.369 --> 00:37:05.216
There's also counseling and psychological
services help, knowing that this is

00:37:05.216 --> 00:37:09.441
going to be a really intensive demanding
experience on multiple fronts.

00:37:09.441 --> 00:37:12.697
And sometimes, it helps to have
someone to talk to, who gets it and

00:37:12.697 --> 00:37:14.690
can help guide you through the process.

00:37:14.690 --> 00:37:18.442
So it's okay to ask for help,
we want you to ask for help, and

00:37:18.442 --> 00:37:22.310
there are multiple spaces
that can be support networks.

00:37:22.310 --> 00:37:26.452
So make sure that maybe even if it's not
the journey you thought it would be.

00:37:26.452 --> 00:37:30.540
We're still making progress and moving
towards whatever goal it is you have.

00:37:30.540 --> 00:37:34.350
So I just want to make sure that's
also risen to the top here.

00:37:34.350 --> 00:37:36.315
&gt;&gt; Great, great, well.

00:37:36.315 --> 00:37:42.479
&gt;&gt; And then kind of full circle,
people are on campus, we have students,

00:37:42.479 --> 00:37:47.849
I know sometimes folks say,
nobody gets in, we have a class.

00:37:47.849 --> 00:37:53.994
We say sometimes, the only surefire way
to not get admitted is to not apply.

00:37:53.994 --> 00:37:56.934
But to that effect, what are some
of the reasons you've heard from

00:37:56.934 --> 00:37:59.466
admitted students about why
they almost didn't apply?

00:37:59.466 --> 00:38:02.478
What were some of the things
that held them back and

00:38:02.478 --> 00:38:05.770
then ultimately they made it through.

00:38:05.770 --> 00:38:08.958
&gt;&gt; It is remarkable will,
and I don't know if it's,

00:38:08.958 --> 00:38:12.306
because we all are having
posteur syndrome at some level.

00:38:12.306 --> 00:38:15.080
[LAUGH] All of us is
just deep in our hearts.

00:38:15.080 --> 00:38:19.204
But when I come to edit weekend, it seems
like one of the most common refrain.

00:38:19.204 --> 00:38:23.170
So they get to meet all our new students
is, Kirsten, I almost didn't apply.

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:27.221
They start with that, and it's for so
many different reasons, so even for

00:38:27.221 --> 00:38:29.358
those who were admitting in the class.

00:38:29.358 --> 00:38:33.246
There's this feeling that it felt,
they had to be courageous to

00:38:33.246 --> 00:38:37.861
be able to share what we're asking him and
actually hit that Submit button.

00:38:37.861 --> 00:38:42.716
But for me, it could be, sometimes people
say, well, I was from consulting or

00:38:42.716 --> 00:38:43.376
finance.

00:38:43.376 --> 00:38:46.729
And everybody applies from that was it,
I just didn't think I'd stick it out.

00:38:46.729 --> 00:38:49.509
Or someone else will say, I'm not from
consulting, I'm not from finance, so

00:38:49.509 --> 00:38:51.263
you're not going to want
to read my application.

00:38:51.263 --> 00:38:54.769
So even that is funny, or
I didn't study business so

00:38:54.769 --> 00:38:59.486
I didn't know whether or not I would
fit in or you would be interested.

00:38:59.486 --> 00:39:02.914
Or I went to a community college or
a State College, not an Ivy League.

00:39:02.914 --> 00:39:07.908
So I didn't think I would have a shot,
what else are, we have one conversation,

00:39:07.908 --> 00:39:09.605
I remember he was a pastor.

00:39:09.605 --> 00:39:12.796
He's like, you wouldn't believe that I
really wanted to be a business person.

00:39:12.796 --> 00:39:17.018
So whatever your imposter is telling
you that little Gremlin voice,

00:39:17.018 --> 00:39:20.311
know that none of these would
be reasons not to apply.

00:39:20.311 --> 00:39:23.695
And many of the things I just talked to
would add to kind of the diversity of your

00:39:23.695 --> 00:39:24.906
perspective in our class.

00:39:24.906 --> 00:39:27.037
Would be something we would
look at as a positive,

00:39:27.037 --> 00:39:29.894
not a negative a positive that you
started at a community college.

00:39:29.894 --> 00:39:35.029
And were able to perhaps transfer or
be able to graduate in the first place.

00:39:35.029 --> 00:39:39.609
So Just don't stop yourself because you're
the only one who can really hit that

00:39:39.609 --> 00:39:40.632
Submit button.

00:39:40.632 --> 00:39:45.174
&gt;&gt; Yeah, take that strength race, strength
based approach, here's what I contribute,

00:39:45.174 --> 00:39:46.030
this is who I am.

00:39:46.030 --> 00:39:46.771
&gt;&gt; I love it, yeah.

00:39:46.771 --> 00:39:51.157
&gt;&gt; And share that with us because
then doors really do open.

00:39:51.157 --> 00:39:54.119
&gt;&gt; And imagine,
imagine me listening to and I say,

00:39:54.119 --> 00:39:57.453
that's a common refrain how
many students I'm like.

00:39:57.453 --> 00:39:59.991
What if these students had not applied?

00:39:59.991 --> 00:40:03.754
It would be I think not only a tragedy for
us in our in our class but

00:40:03.754 --> 00:40:06.446
also the future of- Our world in someways,

00:40:06.446 --> 00:40:10.796
because we're really relying on you to
go out and create this positive impact.

00:40:10.796 --> 00:40:14.816
So you're early in your career, the time
is now to try that, to share yourself and

00:40:14.816 --> 00:40:16.410
take that risk.

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:18.937
&gt;&gt; Yeah, all right, so
we do have some time left.

00:40:18.937 --> 00:40:23.270
I want to make sure we do
jump into that Q&amp;A box.

00:40:23.270 --> 00:40:27.429
So here's the one of the top voted
questions from your non, and

00:40:27.429 --> 00:40:32.533
this is a question about, when it comes
down to two individuals or two files,

00:40:32.533 --> 00:40:37.895
if there's a close match, how do we think
through the decision making process?

00:40:37.895 --> 00:40:42.982
And maybe even just taking it beyond the
hypothetical of, we just have to, what,

00:40:42.982 --> 00:40:48.087
again, just to kind of go over some of the
things we talked about at the beginning.

00:40:48.087 --> 00:40:51.691
What are some of the things that folks can
kind of make sure that they're thinking

00:40:51.691 --> 00:40:54.090
through to put their best foot forward?

00:40:54.090 --> 00:40:57.045
&gt;&gt; Yeah, hey Will,
this is an awesome question, and

00:40:57.045 --> 00:40:59.050
I've been doing a lot of talking.

00:40:59.050 --> 00:41:02.300
So I'm going to switch gears and say,
how about you take a first shot, and I'll

00:41:02.300 --> 00:41:05.752
take a second because I wonder if we're
even a little different than how we might.

00:41:05.752 --> 00:41:10.661
&gt;&gt; Yeah, this is one of those
times where it helps to know

00:41:10.661 --> 00:41:13.944
that there is a committee, right?

00:41:13.944 --> 00:41:17.090
And it's not just one individual
who's making the decision.

00:41:17.090 --> 00:41:21.559
There's multiple folks who are both taking
a look at the individual as well as

00:41:21.559 --> 00:41:23.760
the wholeness of the class.

00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:28.524
And the decision making process is going
to actually change year to year based on

00:41:28.524 --> 00:41:30.560
the context of that class.

00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:36.308
So for me, it's hard to make a clear,
like x, these are the things we look for

00:41:36.308 --> 00:41:40.670
because there's so
many different elements involved.

00:41:40.670 --> 00:41:47.469
It is the holistic review process, so
that is kind of my non-answer, answer.

00:41:47.469 --> 00:41:53.589
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah, it isn't, again,
and it's a tough question, I know.

00:41:53.589 --> 00:41:58.338
Will's right that the decisions are made
within the admissions committee,

00:41:58.338 --> 00:42:02.580
so it's not just one voice who's
looking at any application.

00:42:02.580 --> 00:42:05.988
I know for me though, sometimes I'm trying
to make, even when I'm looking at my own

00:42:05.988 --> 00:42:08.293
pool and trying to make
recommendations on candidates,

00:42:08.293 --> 00:42:09.890
there might be two who feel very close.

00:42:09.890 --> 00:42:14.878
That's part of our job, I tend to go back
in and I'll use what Will said before, for

00:42:14.878 --> 00:42:17.753
this evidence,
we know what our criteria are.

00:42:17.753 --> 00:42:20.649
And where I really go back into
the application again, and

00:42:20.649 --> 00:42:23.606
remind myself of where I've
seen intellectual vitality,

00:42:23.606 --> 00:42:26.570
remind myself of the kinds
of impact someone has had.

00:42:26.570 --> 00:42:30.207
Or who they are as a person and
that helps ground me, and

00:42:30.207 --> 00:42:34.810
oftentimes both individuals could
be really fabulous candidates.

00:42:34.810 --> 00:42:38.154
So I want everyone to know to,
I advise to go wide on

00:42:38.154 --> 00:42:43.320
your MBA applications because luck
isn't maybe the best word to use.

00:42:43.320 --> 00:42:47.310
But we have many more candidates
than we could put in one class.

00:42:47.310 --> 00:42:49.958
So hopefully that makes you feel
better that it's never like,

00:42:49.958 --> 00:42:50.984
I'll just take this one.

00:42:50.984 --> 00:42:52.686
It's always like okay, I need to go in and

00:42:52.686 --> 00:42:54.740
really make sure that this
is the right decision.

00:42:54.740 --> 00:42:57.077
And the same token though,
at any given moment,

00:42:57.077 --> 00:43:00.620
an application could be looked at in
different ways by different people.

00:43:00.620 --> 00:43:04.988
So you guys get the chance to
try to apply to multiple places.

00:43:04.988 --> 00:43:10.060
&gt;&gt; And there's a question here that
I'll actually take Kristen if it's okay.

00:43:10.060 --> 00:43:12.162
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; This is from Daniel, about, yet

00:43:12.162 --> 00:43:13.960
we haven't seen the whole team.

00:43:13.960 --> 00:43:19.275
But the idea of representation is
something that they think about to win,

00:43:19.275 --> 00:43:23.993
but also things that folks from
underrepresented backgrounds,

00:43:23.993 --> 00:43:28.109
that it's really important
to have representation, so

00:43:28.109 --> 00:43:32.420
they feel comfortable in
the application process.

00:43:32.420 --> 00:43:36.433
I see you all are currently hiring
an assistant director for outreach and

00:43:36.433 --> 00:43:37.580
diversity.

00:43:37.580 --> 00:43:40.835
How have you all committed to ensuring
underserved communities have access to

00:43:40.835 --> 00:43:42.080
a degree program like this?

00:43:42.080 --> 00:43:45.653
This and so-
&gt;&gt; And you're [CROSSTALK] hiring actually,

00:43:45.653 --> 00:43:48.691
so send your resume to Will, that's great.

00:43:48.691 --> 00:43:50.120
&gt;&gt; That's actually my own role.

00:43:50.120 --> 00:43:50.817
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And

00:43:50.817 --> 00:43:55.811
I think that's one of the beautiful things
about our office is that even though

00:43:55.811 --> 00:44:00.750
I might be stepping out of that position,
I'm now leading this team.

00:44:00.750 --> 00:44:02.923
That doesn't mean that I
stopped doing that job.

00:44:02.923 --> 00:44:07.875
So now I'm able to help coach, mentor and
support whoever takes on that role,

00:44:07.875 --> 00:44:10.138
and now there's multiple people.

00:44:10.138 --> 00:44:14.411
Programs like application week and really
shifting the way that we think about

00:44:14.411 --> 00:44:18.617
our programming, so that rather than
just telling you about the school, but

00:44:18.617 --> 00:44:23.168
given tools and frameworks for better
understanding of the application process.

00:44:23.168 --> 00:44:26.125
I like to think of that as
closing that equity gap, and

00:44:26.125 --> 00:44:29.950
that piece of equity being like
the knowledge of how to apply.

00:44:29.950 --> 00:44:33.820
Sometimes it can feel like a black box and
if you don't know anybody who hasn't gone

00:44:33.820 --> 00:44:37.540
through it before, who has gone through
it before, who do you turn to for help?

00:44:37.540 --> 00:44:42.952
And so that's one of the things I find so
empowering and motivating about working

00:44:42.952 --> 00:44:48.362
in this office and being able to work with
Kirsten is that, we think a lot about how

00:44:48.362 --> 00:44:53.795
can we make sure this process is as open,
accessible and equitable as possible.

00:44:53.795 --> 00:44:57.581
And so, Danielle, please know and
to everybody out there,

00:44:57.581 --> 00:45:02.555
we take this very seriously and we are
looking to support everyone out there, so

00:45:02.555 --> 00:45:06.937
that you can all have a fair shot and
opportunity to attend a school like

00:45:06.937 --> 00:45:10.520
Stanford or any other institution
that you hope to go to.

00:45:10.520 --> 00:45:12.773
So just wanted to put that plug in there.

00:45:12.773 --> 00:45:14.394
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah and Will, I'll jump in,

00:45:14.394 --> 00:45:17.740
thank you for asking that really
important question, Danielle.

00:45:17.740 --> 00:45:23.005
I also look to the senior leadership
of all of Stanford as well as the GSB.

00:45:23.005 --> 00:45:26.672
And it is one of the highest priorities
of the school right now to make sure

00:45:26.672 --> 00:45:31.180
that our classes represent the diversity
that's out there in our communities.

00:45:31.180 --> 00:45:33.816
And for me to have the honor
of holding this position,

00:45:33.816 --> 00:45:35.407
we do take that very seriously.

00:45:35.407 --> 00:45:40.030
And hopefully you've seen on our web,
we've been much more transparent about

00:45:40.030 --> 00:45:44.377
the backgrounds and the identities of
the students that are coming in, and

00:45:44.377 --> 00:45:49.110
trying with opportunities like this and
Will's broad shoulders and his teams.

00:45:49.110 --> 00:45:52.620
And many others on our team, trying to
increase the number of people in our

00:45:52.620 --> 00:45:56.370
pipelines that people who apply, so
that we can continue to make progress.

00:45:56.370 --> 00:45:59.519
And we have made some pretty dramatic
progress even in the last three years, and

00:45:59.519 --> 00:46:00.696
the diversity of your class.

00:46:00.696 --> 00:46:04.775
And are going to continue to fight,
whether it's gender, ethnicity, and

00:46:04.775 --> 00:46:06.700
all different kinds of diversity.

00:46:06.700 --> 00:46:10.015
So the fact that you're here is
a really phenomenal sign, and

00:46:10.015 --> 00:46:12.650
we're glad you're part
of the audience today.

00:46:12.650 --> 00:46:13.813
And hopefully these and

00:46:13.813 --> 00:46:17.020
other efforts will continue to
make us successful on that front.

00:46:17.020 --> 00:46:20.043
because that's my job, I want the
boardrooms of tomorrow to look like what

00:46:20.043 --> 00:46:23.180
our communities are, not to look like
what they've looked in the past.

00:46:24.190 --> 00:46:27.930
&gt;&gt; Right, Kristen, the time flew by,
we have a couple of minutes.

00:46:27.930 --> 00:46:30.258
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; We can close out with advice,

00:46:30.258 --> 00:46:35.068
words of wisdom or words of encouragement
that you have for the group,

00:46:35.068 --> 00:46:40.060
I think that would be an amazing
way to close out application week.

00:46:40.060 --> 00:46:44.190
&gt;&gt; All right, I will do this though,
will only if you will close after my close

00:46:44.190 --> 00:46:46.760
because you are one of
the heroes of this week.

00:46:46.760 --> 00:46:49.703
I have such appreciation, so
it'd be wonderful you do the final wrap,

00:46:49.703 --> 00:46:50.378
is that fair?

00:46:50.378 --> 00:46:52.920
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] All right.

00:46:52.920 --> 00:46:57.760
All right, so, I do this job,
fundamentally because I believe that each

00:46:57.760 --> 00:47:02.287
of us has potential, and
many times we aren't given the resources,

00:47:02.287 --> 00:47:07.920
the confidence, even the self narrative
to know what we're capable of.

00:47:07.920 --> 00:47:10.797
And one of the things that I'm
most proud about at Stanford is,

00:47:10.797 --> 00:47:14.280
I think our program is designed to
help people understand who they are.

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:19.089
And again, get that confidence to really
dream big, and then upon graduation,

00:47:19.089 --> 00:47:21.650
take the risks to make that happen.

00:47:21.650 --> 00:47:26.547
So for anyone out in the audience who
does have things they believe that

00:47:26.547 --> 00:47:30.614
they're capable of that
are bigger than themselves, and

00:47:30.614 --> 00:47:35.179
for anyone out there who might
feel like but, I don't know,

00:47:35.179 --> 00:47:40.089
it's time to take a step, and
we put on this application week for you.

00:47:40.089 --> 00:47:43.556
If you don't believe in yourself and
begin there, no one else can or will.

00:47:43.556 --> 00:47:47.769
But know that Will and myself and the rest
of our admissions team is looking forward

00:47:47.769 --> 00:47:51.616
to learning more about you, to honoring
you, and no matter what happens,

00:47:51.616 --> 00:47:54.790
it is a step for you to start
owning what you're capable of.

00:47:54.790 --> 00:47:59.068
So, good luck and
I can't wait to learn more about you, and

00:47:59.068 --> 00:48:01.722
you Will [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; To bring this home,

00:48:01.722 --> 00:48:06.690
I think Stanford is a place
where people see possibility.

00:48:06.690 --> 00:48:09.518
They see possibility in themselves and
each other,

00:48:09.518 --> 00:48:13.696
the way that we're going to be able to
tackle the world's biggest problems,

00:48:13.696 --> 00:48:17.560
it's like yes, we can and
we're going to do this together.

00:48:17.560 --> 00:48:21.298
And I think this is encapsulated
on the cornerstone, so

00:48:21.298 --> 00:48:26.890
I don't have the inscription up in front
of me, so hopefully I get it right.

00:48:26.890 --> 00:48:30.605
But it's dedicated to the things
that haven't happened yet, and

00:48:30.605 --> 00:48:33.070
to the people who
are about to dream them up.

00:48:34.090 --> 00:48:37.669
This is a place that's dedicated to you,
your dreams, your ideas,

00:48:37.669 --> 00:48:41.249
the things that you want to do,
the things that haven't happened yet

00:48:41.249 --> 00:48:42.995
that you might be the pioneer of.

00:48:42.995 --> 00:48:46.631
Whether it is a new field or maybe
just the pioneer of tackling that new

00:48:46.631 --> 00:48:49.780
little problem that you've
had that's been bugging you.

00:48:49.780 --> 00:48:52.060
And now let's look at it
from a new direction,

00:48:52.060 --> 00:48:55.830
this is a place where we want
you to dream big, lead boldly.

00:48:55.830 --> 00:49:02.333
So if there's one thing that I could say
to all of you, it's dream big, y'all.

00:49:02.333 --> 00:49:07.269
Thank you so much for joining us,
we hope you had a great time this week,

00:49:07.269 --> 00:49:08.900
I know I did.

00:49:08.900 --> 00:49:12.098
And we'll hope to see you soon, take care.

00:49:12.098 --> 00:49:15.241
And from the Stanford Graduate School
of Business, we hope you

00:49:15.241 --> 00:49:18.988
have a wonderful rest of the day, and
we hope to see your application soon.

00:49:21.395 --> 00:49:25.978
[MUSIC]

